```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const Autocomplete = ({itaminOptions, placeholder, value, onChange}) => {
  const [optimizations, setOptimizations] = useState(itaminOptions);
  const [filteredOptions, setFilteredOptions] = useState(itaminOptions);
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    setOptimizations(itaminOptions);
    setFilteredOptions(itaminOptions);
  }, [itaminOptions]);

  const handleInputChange = (event) => {
    const newValue = event.target.value;
    const lowerCaseValue = newValue.toLowerCase();
    setInputValue(lowerCaseValue);

    setFilteredOptions(itaminOptions.filter((option) => {
      return option.toLowerCase().startsWith(lowerCaseValue);
    }));
  };

  const handleSelectOption = (option) => {
    setInputValue(option);
    setFilteredOptions(itaminOptions);
    if (onChange) {
      onChange(option);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={inputValue}
        onChange={handleInputChange}
        placeholder={placeholder}
      />
      <ul>
        {filteredOptions.map((option, index) => (
          <li key={index} onClick={() => handleSelectOption(option)}>
            {option}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Autocomplete;
```

This component takes four properties:

- `itaminOptions`: an array of options to autocomplete
- `placeholder`: the placeholder text for the input field
- `value`: the value of the input field
- `onChange`: an optional function to be called when an option is selected

It uses four state variables:

- `optimizations`: the original array of options
- `filteredOptions`: an array of options to be filtered
- `inputValue`: the current value of the input field
- `inputSuggestions`: an array of suggestions

The component renders a text input field and a list of options. When the user types something in the input field, the component filters the options array and updates the `filteredOptions` array with options that start with the input value. When the user clicks on an option, the component updates the `inputValue` state to the selected option and calls the `onChange` function if it is provided.

