Here is a simple Todo List app in React that includes the functionality to add, remove, and mark tasks as completed:
```
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import './App.css';

function App() {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState([]);
  const [newTask, setNewTask] = useState('');

  const handleAddTask = () => {
    setTasks([...tasks, { text: newTask, completed: false }]);
    setNewTask('');
  };

  const handleRemoveTask = (index) => {
    setTasks(tasks.filter((task, i) => i !== index));
  };

  const handleMarkComplete = (index) => {
    setTasks(
      tasks.map((task, i) => {
        if (i === index) {
          return { ...task, completed: !task.completed };
        } else {
          return task;
        }
      })
    );
  };

  return (
    <div className="App">
      <h1>Todo List: {tasks.length} tasks</h1>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={newTask}
        onChange={(e) => setNewTask(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Enter a new task"
      />
      <button onClick={handleAddTask}>Add Task</button>
      <ul>
        {tasks.map((task, index) => (
          <li key={index}>
            <input
              type="checkbox"
              checked={task.completed}
              onChange={() => handleMarkComplete(index)}
            />
            <span
              style={{
                textDecoration: task.completed ? 'line-through' : 'none',
              }}
            >
              {task.text}
            </span>
            <button onClick={() => handleRemoveTask(index)}>X</button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```
Let me explain what each part of the code does:

* `useState` is used to create two state variables: `tasks` (an array of tasks) and `newTask` (a string to hold the new task being added).
* The `handleAddTask` function adds a new task to the `tasks` array and clears the `newTask` input field.
* The `handleRemoveTask` function removes a task from the `tasks` array by filtering out the task at the specified index.
* The `handleMarkComplete` function toggles the `completed` property of a task at the specified index.
* The rest of the code renders a basic UI with an input field, a button to add tasks, and a list of tasks. Each task has a checkbox to mark it as completed, and an "X" button to remove it.

